Network Devices:
In this session, I will discuss the types of physical devices we use to establish our network and enable nodes to communicate effectively.
For a cybersecurity or ethical hacker, it is very important to know how data travels between different devices or networks.
1. Router: A physical device that routes our traffic to access data from outside(from other LA parts or the Internet) of the network. It decides the best way to travel with data efficiently. e.g., we use a router in a LAN to access the WAN.
2. NIC: (Network Interface Card)This is a device or card that gives a system a particular address (while manufacturing), which is used to get data from other devices and send data to other devices or nodes using this address. Modern devices have built-in NICs.
3. HUB: This particular device is used to send and receive data between different nodes within a network. The drawback of this device is that when we send a data segment to a particular node, it sends this same data to each node in the network; we get a huge amount of traffic between devices, causing a slowdown in the network. To control this traffic, we use switches nowadays.
4. Switch: This is actually a HUB, but it sends and receives data between nodes intelligently. A switch has the address of each node in the network. When we send data to a particular node, it will match the address and only allow the corresponding device to receive data. This feature will help us to keep the traffic controlled within a network.
      There are further types of switches.
         1. Circuit Switching: In Circuit Switching, we turn a full circuit into a path for sending data between just the sender and receiver. During this process, a dedicated path has been established between the sender and the destination address. No one else uses this path until they cut their connection.
         2. Message Switching: In this type, a network uses a store-and-send technique in which the whole data set is first stored in the switch and then sent to the destination address entirely at a time.
         3. Packet Switching: In this technique, data travels between the sender and receiver by creating small segments of data. Each data packet receives the destination address and begins moving without considering the other data packets. 
      There are two types of packet switching further: 
         1. Connectionless or datagram approach: During this method, data packets start to move just by receiving the destination address. Every packet of data uses a different path in the network to reach the destination address. 
         2. Connection-oriented or virtual circuit: During this method, a connection has been established between the sender and receiver. A virtual path has been created, and each data packet should follow this exact path to reach the destination address.
5. Bridge: This is used to connect two different LANs so they can also send and receive data within two or more networks. We usually use it normally to connect two LAN networks.
6. Firewalls: This acts as a security element for our network. This decides which device is allowed to connect to a network and which is not. We use it to establish secure communication within a network. 
7. Repeaters: Which is used to keep the signals as it is within a network. This acts as a signal booster, which means when we want to increase our wireless LAN range, so when the signals get weak, the repeaters catch these signals and boost them again so the other far devices get the same speed or data Rransfer Rate.
8. APs: Stands for Access Points, which means when we want to connect our main devices like computers, servers, and printers with wires, but we also want the other devices to also access the same network using wireless technology, then we use the APs with our wired router or switch. This creates a wireless broadcast that allows the other devices to connect to the same network.
9. Modem: For modulation and demodulation. This is used to convert the computer signals into readable analogue signals for our ISP so we can access the Internet.
10. Gateways: This is the entrance point for each network, which is used to translate the protocols. This is most important when we connect our network to another network, which is based on a communication protocol that is entirely different. We can also say this as a translator that translates the traffic of a network according to other network communication protocols. It can be a dedicated physical device or software that is installed in a computer.
